from full_a import cat, C

cat("family", "Jesus & His Family", "The family God chose for His Son.", "nativity", [
 dict(id="jesus", name="Jesus", role="The Son of God, who became a man", art="sermon",
  facts=["Born in Bethlehem", "Grew up in Nazareth", "Died and rose again"], cards=[
  C("The Word became flesh", "Jesus is the eternal Son of God. He existed before the world began, and about 2,000 years ago He became a human being.",
    "John’s Gospel opens: “In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God… The Word became flesh and made His dwelling among us.” He is fully God and fully man — not half of each. He was born a real baby, grew up, got hungry and tired, wept, and died a real death. His name, Yeshua in Hebrew, means “the Lord saves.” “Christ” is not a last name but a title — the Greek for Messiah, “the anointed one,” the King God had promised for centuries.", ("John",1)),
  C("Born in Bethlehem", "Jesus was born to the virgin Mary in Bethlehem, the town of King David, and laid in a manger. Angels announced it to shepherds.",
    "A Roman census forced Joseph and Mary to travel about 90 miles from Nazareth to Bethlehem, Joseph’s ancestral town — fulfilling the prophet Micah, who said the ruler of Israel would come from Bethlehem. There was no space in the guest room, so the newborn was laid in a feeding trough. That night an angel appeared to shepherds outside town: “Today in the town of David a Savior has been born to you; He is the Messiah, the Lord.” A crowd of angels filled the sky praising God. The shepherds hurried to find the baby and spread the news.", ("Luke",2)),
  C("Wise men and a wicked king", "Wise men from the East followed a star to worship Him. King Herod tried to kill Him, and the family fled to Egypt.",
    "Magi — scholars who studied the stars — arrived in Jerusalem asking where the newborn King of the Jews was. King Herod, paranoid about rivals, pretended he wanted to worship too. The star led the Magi to the house in Bethlehem, where they gave gold, frankincense and myrrh. Warned in a dream, they went home another way. An angel warned Joseph to flee to Egypt. Herod then ordered every boy two years old and under in Bethlehem killed. After Herod died, the family returned and settled in Nazareth.", ("Matthew",2)),
  C("Twelve years old in the Temple", "At 12, Jesus stayed behind in Jerusalem. His parents found Him in the Temple amazing the teachers: “I had to be in My Father’s house.”",
    "It’s the only story we have of Jesus’ childhood. His family traveled in a big group of relatives for Passover, so Mary and Joseph went a whole day before realizing He wasn’t with them. After three days of searching they found Him sitting with the teachers, listening and asking questions. Mary said, “Your father and I have been anxiously searching for You.” He answered, “Didn’t you know I had to be in My Father’s house?” Then He went home and obeyed them, and grew in wisdom and stature, and in favor with God and people. He worked as a carpenter until about age 30.", ("Luke",2)),
  C("Baptized and tested", "At about 30, Jesus was baptized by John. Heaven opened and God said, “This is My Son.” Then He fasted 40 days and beat Satan’s temptations.",
    "As He came up out of the Jordan, the Spirit came down like a dove and the Father’s voice spoke — Father, Son and Spirit all present. Then the Spirit led Him into the desert. After 40 days without food, Satan tempted Him three times: turn stones into bread, jump from the Temple to prove God would catch Him, and bow down to get all the kingdoms of the world. Each time Jesus answered with Scripture: “Man shall not live on bread alone,” “Do not put the Lord your God to the test,” “Worship the Lord your God and serve Him only.” Where Adam failed in a garden, Jesus held firm in a wilderness.", ("Matthew",4)),
  C("Water into wine", "His first miracle was at a wedding in Cana, turning about 150 gallons of water into the best wine of the party.",
    "When the wine ran out, Mary told Jesus, then told the servants, “Do whatever He tells you.” He had them fill six stone jars used for ritual washing — each holding 20 to 30 gallons — with water. When the master of the banquet tasted it, it was wine, and better than what had been served first. John calls it the first of His signs, revealing His glory, and His disciples believed in Him.", ("John",2)),
  C("Calling the twelve", "Jesus called fishermen, a tax collector and a rebel to follow Him, and after a night of prayer chose twelve to be His apostles.",
    "He called Peter, Andrew, James and John from their boats and Matthew from his tax booth. He chose twelve — matching the twelve tribes of Israel — to be with Him, to preach and to drive out demons. They were mostly ordinary, uneducated working men. He trained them for about three years by living with them.", ("Luke",6)),
  C("The Sermon on the Mount", "On a hillside He taught the Beatitudes, the Lord’s Prayer, “love your enemies,” and the Golden Rule.",
    "“Blessed are the poor in spirit… blessed are those who mourn… blessed are the meek… blessed are the peacemakers.” He taught that anger is like murder in the heart and lust is like adultery. Instead of revenge: turn the other cheek. Pray for those who persecute you. Don’t worry about tomorrow — look at the birds; your Father feeds them. Do to others what you want them to do to you. He ended with the wise man who built his house on the rock. The crowds were amazed, because He taught as one with authority.", ("Matthew",5)),
  C("Healing the hurting", "Jesus healed lepers, the blind, the paralyzed, and a woman bleeding for 12 years. He drove out demons and raised the dead.",
    "He touched a man with leprosy — something no one did — and he was clean. Four friends lowered a paralyzed man through a roof, and Jesus first said, “Your sins are forgiven,” then told him to walk. A woman bleeding for twelve years touched His cloak and was healed. He opened blind eyes and deaf ears. He raised Jairus’s daughter, a widow’s only son at Nain, and His friend Lazarus after four days in the tomb. The miracles showed His power and His compassion — and pointed to who He is.", ("Mark",2)),
  C("Lord of the storm", "When a storm nearly sank the boat, Jesus told the wind and waves, “Quiet! Be still!” — and they obeyed.",
    "Jesus was asleep on a cushion in the back of the boat while it filled with water. The terrified disciples woke Him: “Teacher, don’t You care if we drown?” He got up, rebuked the wind and the sea, and there was a dead calm. Then He asked, “Why are you so afraid? Do you still have no faith?” They were more terrified than before: “Who is this? Even the wind and the waves obey Him!” On another night He walked across the lake to them.", ("Mark",4)),
  C("Feeding the 5,000", "With five loaves and two fish, Jesus fed over 5,000 people, with twelve baskets left over. It’s the only miracle in all four Gospels.",
    "The count was 5,000 men, besides women and children. He had them sit on the green grass in groups, gave thanks, broke the bread and kept giving it to the disciples to hand out. The next day He told the crowd, “I am the bread of life. Whoever comes to Me will never go hungry.” Many followers found that teaching too hard and left. Jesus asked the twelve, “Do you want to leave too?” Peter answered, “Lord, to whom would we go? You have the words of eternal life.”", ("John",6)),
  C("Stories that stick", "Jesus taught in parables: the Good Samaritan, the Prodigal Son, the Lost Sheep, the Sower, the mustard seed.",
    "A parable is a short, everyday story with a deeper point. When a lawyer asked “Who is my neighbor?”, Jesus told about a Samaritan — a despised outsider — who stopped to help a beaten man that religious leaders walked past. He told about a shepherd who leaves 99 sheep to find one, and a father who runs to welcome home a son who wasted everything. The kingdom of God, He said, is like a tiny mustard seed that grows into a tree, or like treasure hidden in a field worth selling everything for.", ("Luke",15)),
  C("Friend of sinners", "Jesus ate with tax collectors, defended a woman caught in adultery, and welcomed children. The religious leaders were furious.",
    "He went to Zacchaeus’s house, let a sinful woman wash His feet with her tears, and said to a woman dragged before Him for adultery, “Neither do I condemn you. Go and sin no more.” When disciples shooed children away, He said, “Let the little children come to Me.” His critics called Him “a friend of tax collectors and sinners.” He said, “The Son of Man came to seek and to save the lost.”", ("Luke",19)),
  C("Who do you say I am?", "Peter declared He was the Messiah. Jesus began telling them plainly He would be killed and rise on the third day.",
    "Jesus made claims no ordinary teacher would make: “I am the light of the world,” “I am the good shepherd who lays down His life for the sheep,” “I am the resurrection and the life,” “Before Abraham was, I am” — using God’s own name. He forgave sins, which only God can do. Three times He predicted His death and resurrection. The disciples didn’t understand.", ("Matthew",16)),
  C("Palm Sunday", "Jesus rode into Jerusalem on a donkey as crowds waved palm branches and shouted, “Hosanna!” Then He drove the money changers out of the Temple.",
    "The prophet Zechariah had said Israel’s king would come “humble and riding on a donkey.” Crowds spread cloaks and branches on the road. Days later He overturned the tables of merchants and money changers in the Temple courts: “My house will be called a house of prayer, but you are making it a den of robbers.” The chief priests began looking for a way to kill Him, but were afraid of the crowds.", ("Matthew",21)),
  C("The Last Supper", "At Passover, Jesus washed His disciples’ feet, shared bread and wine as His body and blood, and said one of them would betray Him.",
    "He took bread: “This is My body, given for you; do this in remembrance of Me.” He took the cup: “This cup is the new covenant in My blood, poured out for you.” He gave them a new command: “Love one another as I have loved you.” He promised to send the Holy Spirit and prayed for them and for all who would believe through them.", ("Luke",22)),
  C("Gethsemane", "In a garden at night, Jesus prayed in agony: “Not My will, but Yours be done.” Then Judas arrived with soldiers.",
    "He was so distressed His sweat was like drops of blood. Three times He found the disciples sleeping. Judas identified Him with a kiss. Peter swung a sword; Jesus healed the wounded servant and let Himself be arrested. All the disciples fled.", ("Luke",22)),
  C("On trial", "Jesus was tried overnight by the Jewish council, then before Pontius Pilate and Herod. Pilate found no guilt, but gave in to the crowd.",
    "False witnesses contradicted each other. The high priest finally asked, “Are You the Messiah, the Son of the Blessed One?” Jesus said, “I am,” and they condemned Him for blasphemy. Since only Rome could execute, they took Him to Pilate, accusing Him of claiming to be a king. Pilate said, “I find no basis for a charge.” The crowd demanded the release of Barabbas, a murderer, instead, and shouted, “Crucify Him!” Pilate had Jesus flogged, soldiers crowned Him with thorns, and Pilate handed Him over.", ("John",18)),
  C("The cross", "Jesus was crucified between two criminals. He forgave His executioners and died, taking the punishment for our sins.",
    "Around nine in the morning they nailed Him to a cross on Golgotha. Above Him was a sign: “Jesus of Nazareth, King of the Jews.” He prayed, “Father, forgive them, for they do not know what they are doing.” He promised a dying thief, “Today you will be with Me in paradise.” Darkness covered the land from noon until three. He cried, “My God, My God, why have You forsaken Me?” then, “It is finished,” and “Father, into Your hands I commit My spirit.” The Temple curtain tore from top to bottom. Isaiah had written: “He was pierced for our transgressions… by His wounds we are healed.”", ("Luke",23)),
  C("He is risen", "On Sunday morning the tomb was empty. Jesus rose from the dead and appeared to Mary Magdalene, the apostles, and over 500 people.",
    "Women came at dawn with spices and found the stone rolled away. Angels said, “Why do you look for the living among the dead? He is not here; He has risen!” He appeared to Mary Magdalene in the garden, to two disciples walking to Emmaus, to the apostles behind locked doors, to Thomas, and to seven of them by the lake. He ate with them and showed them His wounds. Paul, writing about 25 years later, lists the witnesses — including over 500 at once, most of them still alive to be asked.", ("Luke",24)),
  C("Ascended — and coming back", "After 40 days, Jesus gave His followers their mission and was taken up into heaven. He promised to return.",
    "“All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to Me. Go and make disciples of all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit… and surely I am with you always, to the very end of the age.” Then He was taken up before their eyes, and two angels said, “This same Jesus will come back in the same way you have seen Him go into heaven.” Ten days later the Holy Spirit came at Pentecost, and the church began.", ("Acts",1)),
 ]),
 dict(id="mary", name="Mary", role="The young woman who said yes to God", art="annunciation",
  facts=["Mother of Jesus", "From Nazareth", "Was at the cross"], cards=[
  C("Engaged in Nazareth", "Mary was a young Jewish woman, probably a teenager, engaged to a carpenter named Joseph in the small village of Nazareth.",
    "Jewish engagement was a legally binding commitment — breaking it required a divorce — but the couple didn’t live together yet. Mary was a relative of Elizabeth, the wife of a priest. Her name in Hebrew is Miriam, after Moses’ sister.", ("Luke",1)),
  C("The angel Gabriel", "Gabriel told her she would conceive by the Holy Spirit and bear the Son of God. She said: “I am the Lord’s servant.”",
    "Gabriel greeted her: “Rejoice, you who are highly favored! The Lord is with you.” She was troubled. He said she would have a son named Jesus who would reign on David’s throne forever. She asked, “How will this be, since I am a virgin?” He said the Holy Spirit would come upon her and the power of the Most High would overshadow her. “Nothing is impossible with God.” Mary answered, “May it be to me according to your word.” She knew an unexplained pregnancy could cost her her engagement, her reputation, even her life.", ("Luke",1)),
  C("Visiting Elizabeth", "Mary hurried to see her relative Elizabeth. The baby in Elizabeth’s womb leaped, and Mary sang a song of praise — the Magnificat.",
    "Elizabeth, pregnant with John the Baptist in her old age, was filled with the Holy Spirit and cried out, “Blessed are you among women, and blessed is the fruit of your womb!” Mary’s song begins, “My soul magnifies the Lord, and my spirit rejoices in God my Savior.” It praises God who lifts up the humble and fills the hungry. She stayed about three months.", ("Luke",1)),
  C("The birth", "In Bethlehem, Mary gave birth to Jesus and laid Him in a manger. When shepherds told what the angels said, she treasured it all in her heart.",
    "Forty days later, at the Temple, an old man named Simeon took the baby in his arms and praised God, then told Mary: “A sword will pierce your own soul too.” Then came the Magi, the escape to Egypt, and the return to Nazareth.", ("Luke",2)),
  C("“Do whatever He tells you”", "At a wedding in Cana, Mary told Jesus the wine had run out, then told the servants, “Do whatever He tells you.”",
    "Those are her last recorded words in John’s Gospel — and good advice for anyone. Jesus turned the water into wine. Mary also appears during His ministry, once coming with His brothers to take Him home because people said He was out of His mind.", ("John",2)),
  C("At the cross", "Mary stood by the cross as her Son died. Jesus entrusted her to John: “Here is your mother.”",
    "Simeon’s prophecy of a sword piercing her soul came true. Even in agony, Jesus made sure she would be cared for, and John took her into his home.", ("John",19)),
  C("With the church", "After the resurrection, Mary was praying with the apostles in Jerusalem when the Holy Spirit came at Pentecost.",
    "Acts names “Mary the mother of Jesus” among those praying constantly in the upper room — the last time the Bible mentions her. Catholic and Orthodox Christians honor her as the Mother of God (Theotokos), since the child she bore is God the Son.", ("Acts",1)),
 ]),
 dict(id="joseph-nazareth", name="Joseph of Nazareth", role="The carpenter who raised Jesus", art="nativity",
  facts=["Mary’s husband", "A carpenter", "Never says a word in the Bible"], cards=[
  C("A righteous man", "Joseph was a carpenter in Nazareth, a descendant of King David, engaged to Mary.",
    "The Greek word for his trade, tekton, means a builder or craftsman working in wood or stone. Matthew’s Gospel traces his family line back through King David to Abraham, which is how Jesus legally became “Son of David.”", ("Matthew",1)),
  C("The dream", "When Mary was pregnant, Joseph planned to end the engagement quietly. An angel told him in a dream: marry her — the child is from God.",
    "Joseph was “a righteous man and did not want to expose her to public disgrace,” so he planned a quiet divorce. Then an angel said, “Do not be afraid to take Mary home as your wife… She will give birth to a son, and you are to give Him the name Jesus, because He will save His people from their sins.” When Joseph woke up, he did exactly what the angel said.", ("Matthew",1)),
  C("Protector", "Joseph took Mary to Bethlehem, then woke in the night and fled with them to Egypt to save the baby from Herod.",
    "Joseph received four dreams in Matthew’s Gospel and obeyed every one immediately: marry Mary, flee to Egypt, return to Israel, and settle in Galilee instead of Judea. He named Jesus, presented Him at the Temple, and taught Him his trade.", ("Matthew",2)),
  C("Searching for Jesus", "When 12-year-old Jesus stayed behind in Jerusalem, Joseph and Mary searched for three days.",
    "This is the last time Joseph appears alive in the Gospels. He was likely dead before Jesus’ ministry began, since he isn’t mentioned at the cross, where Jesus entrusted Mary to John. Joseph never speaks a single recorded word in the Bible.", ("Luke",2)),
 ]),
 dict(id="john-baptist", name="John the Baptist", role="The wild prophet who prepared the way for Jesus", art="baptist",
  facts=["Jesus’ relative", "Lived in the desert", "Baptized Jesus"], cards=[
  C("A miracle baby", "The angel Gabriel told the elderly priest Zechariah his wife Elizabeth would have a son. He doubted, and was struck mute until John was born.",
    "Zechariah was serving in the Temple when Gabriel appeared and said the boy would go before the Lord “in the spirit and power of Elijah.” Because Zechariah asked for proof, he couldn’t speak for nine months. When relatives wanted to name the baby after his father, Zechariah wrote on a tablet, “His name is John,” and his voice came back.", ("Luke",1)),
  C("The voice in the desert", "John lived in the wilderness, wore camel hair, ate locusts and wild honey, and preached: “Repent — the kingdom of heaven is near!”",
    "Crowds came out from Jerusalem and all Judea. He told soldiers not to extort money, tax collectors not to overcharge, and anyone with two coats to share. He called the religious leaders a “brood of vipers.” Isaiah had foretold him: “A voice of one calling in the wilderness, ‘Prepare the way for the Lord.’”", ("Matthew",3)),
  C("“Behold, the Lamb of God”", "John baptized Jesus in the Jordan and pointed his own disciples to Him: “Look, the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world!”",
    "At first John tried to stop Jesus: “I need to be baptized by You!” When some of John’s followers worried that everyone was going to Jesus now, John said, “He must become greater; I must become less.”", ("John",1)),
  C("Doubts in prison", "Jailed by Herod Antipas, John sent messengers to ask Jesus: “Are You the one, or should we expect someone else?”",
    "Jesus answered by pointing to what was happening: the blind see, the lame walk, lepers are cleansed, the dead are raised, and the poor hear good news. Then He told the crowd that no one born of a woman was greater than John.", ("Matthew",11)),
  C("How it ended", "John told Herod it was wrong to marry his brother’s wife. At a birthday party, Herod had him beheaded.",
    "Herodias’s daughter danced for Herod and his guests, and he promised her anything. Prompted by her mother, she asked for John’s head on a platter. Herod was distressed but didn’t want to break his oath in front of guests. The Jewish historian Josephus also records John’s execution by Herod.", ("Mark",6)),
 ]),
])
